WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:19.680 position:64% line:92%
This is a story about recycling gone awry

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.650 position:58% line:92%
and how claims of sustainability from

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:31.780 position:61%
large companies are going up in flames.


00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.060 position:59%
Mustafa Nagar is a city 80 miles north
of New Delhi.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.240 position:58%
The surrounding area is known for its
sugar and paper mills.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:01:00.180 position:63%
Now it's taken on a much darker claim to
fame as a dumping ground for ton after

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:05.129 position:61%
ton of American plastic.
This field of plastic is right behind a

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:07.350
village.
There are children playing nearby.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.950 position:59%
We've walked through it.
And surprisingly, most of this plastic

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.410 position:56%
comes from overseas, mostly from the
U.S.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.110
and Canada.
And one brand stands out.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:21.510 position:61%
Amazon. Literally,
there are Amazon shipping bags all over

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.750 position:63%
this field.
How did this plastic waste from overseas

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:29.640 position:63%
get here?
Paper mills rely on imported waste paper

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:34.200 position:61%
because it's cheaper than wood pulp.
And India can't source enough paper for

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.450 position:58%
recycling domestically.
The problem is that plastic packaging

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.270 position:59%
waste is often bundled with this waste
paper.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.360 position:59%
It's estimated that as much as five
hundred thousand tons of plastic waste

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:55.120 position:61%
is coming into India every year.
Much of that plastic ends up being used

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.170 position:63%
as fueled by local industries, including
paper and sugar mills.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.570 position:63%
Residents say they know when plastic was
burned overnight because they usually

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:09.490 position:63%
wake up to a layer of gray and black ash
that coats, terraces, crops and anything

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:15.870
left outdoors.
Vilma Devi is a longtime resident

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:20.190 position:63%
who's developed health problems in the
last few years because of pollution from

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:25.550
burning plastic


00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:37.630 line:92%
We're surrounded by paper

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:42.940 position:97% line:92%
mills here in the city of Muzaffarnagar. Plumes of thick black smoke are billowing

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.790 position:69%
from these facilities all around us.
We don't know for sure what they're burning.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:50.410 position:61%
But experts say black smoke is one
indication that plastic's being burned.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:03:00.130 position:61%
Rahul Kumar is an activist in a village
in Muzaffarnagar.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:05.950 position:58%
He leads a group of eight volunteers
working to raise awareness of air and

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:11.860 position:63%
water pollution caused by the industrial
burning of plastic.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:29.410 position:59%
Recycling is supposed to cut pollution
and give valuable materials a second

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:32.560 position:59%
life.
But here the global recycling system's

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:38.050 position:63%
failings are hard to deny.
Labels reveal just how far the waste has

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.450
traveled.
There's plastic trash from Nestlé,

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:45.340 position:61%
Costco's Kirkland and Under Armour, all
thrown out by U.S.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.260
and Canadian consumers.
Some 7000 miles away.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.110 position:63%
Still, some paper industry executives in
Muzaffarnagar say the plastic that comes

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:59.800 position:63%
with paper recycling isn't a problem.
Pankaj Aggarwal runs a paper mill and is

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.590
chairman of the Paper Manufacturers
Association for the State of Uttar

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:06.760
Pradesh.
This is imported.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:11.300
This is imported.


00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.470 position:61%
This is a mixed waste, but there is not
much contamination.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:23.700
You can see there is no plastic
At a dump site close to Aggarwal's

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:28.230 position:58%
paper mill, one plastic envelope with
a United States Postal Service label

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.440
stood out.
It had a name and address printed

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.950 position:56%
directly on it.
It had been shipped to Laurie Smyla,

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.110 position:63%
who we later found out was a 73 year old
retiree from Sloatsburg in Rockland

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.210
County, New York.
We tracked her down from India.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.890
Laurie, it's Oanh Ha from Bloomberg
News.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.560
Thanks so much for joining us from
upstate New York.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.410 position:63%
So I found something that used to belong
to you.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:59.090 position:63%
This is a United States Postal Service
envelope that arrived in your house once

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:01.670
and we found it in this field.
What do you make of it?

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:07.600 position:63%
It's phenomenal because I know that I do
receive packages on a regular basis.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.140 position:61%
I'm sure that one contained something I
ordered.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.800 position:61%
The question is how in the world did it
end up

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:18.570 position:56%
in India?
Well, we as consumers, we think that

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:20.820 position:63%
when we put something into the recycling
bin.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.000 position:58%
I mean, what's the expectation there?
And I'm wondering how you feel about

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.150 position:63%
that.
For years, we've thought that everything

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:31.620 position:56%
that we're doing is the right thing.
But obviously, that wasn't the case.

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:37.140 position:63%
I feel sorry for anyone who lives within
a five mile radius of the site you're

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:44.360 position:61%
standing on.
Amazon wouldn't comment on the presence

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:48.620 position:61%
of its packaging inMuzzafarnagar, but
in a statement, the company said it was

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:52.400
committed to minimizing waste and
helping customers recycle their

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.500
